  "slug": "write-a-knowledge-base-ownership-rule-for-an-offshore-team",
  "title": "Write a Knowledge Base Ownership Rule for an Offshore Team",
  "excerpt": "Assign one accountable owner to every article, define review triggers, and keep the knowledge base usable after people change roles.",
  "minutes": 9,
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-09",
  "image": "/daily09/offshoreresourcing/write-a-knowledge-base-ownership-rule-for-an-offshore-team.webp",
  "details": {
   "shortAnswer": ["A knowledge base ownership rule names one accountable person for each article, separates drafting from approval, and sets the events that force a review. It also records the source behind each claim and defines what happens when an owner leaves. Without that rule, a large library becomes a pile of documents that nobody trusts. Use [offshore staffing services](/services) and the [research library](/research) to connect the rule to role design and evidence standards. Publish the ownership map where the team works, not only in a project tracker."],
   "takeaways": ["Give every article one named owner and one named approver.", "Review on triggers such as a policy change, not only on a calendar.", "Record the source and the last verification date on the article.", "Reassign ownership before a departure, not after access is removed."],
   "sections": [
    {
     "heading": "Give every article one named owner",
     "paragraphs": ["Shared ownership sounds collaborative but usually means nobody is accountable. Assign each article to one person who keeps it accurate, answers questions about it, and starts a review when something changes. The owner does not have to write every edit, but the owner must confirm that the final text is correct. Record the owner on the article itself, not only in a separate spreadsheet that drifts out of date. Use a person rather than a team name, because a team cannot notice that its own article has become wrong."]
    },
    {
     "heading": "Separate writing from approval",
     "paragraphs": ["The person who drafts an update should not be the only person who approves it when the article affects customer commitments, money, or access. Name an approver who can check the claim against the source and the current policy. For low-risk internal notes, the owner may approve their own edit, but the rule should say so explicitly. Record the approver and the approval date beside the article. This is not bureaucracy for its own sake. It prevents a plausible rewrite from quietly changing a threshold or a step that the team relies on."]
    },
    {
     "heading": "Set a review trigger list",
     "paragraphs": ["A calendar-only review cycle lets important articles go stale between dates. Add triggers that force a review when the policy, system, client, or owner changes. A new approval limit, a renamed field, a different escalation contact, or a repeated question from the queue should all start a review. Give the owner a short window to confirm, revise, or retire the article. If the owner does not respond, route the item to the approver rather than letting it sit. The trigger list should be short enough that the team can remember it during a normal week."]
    },
    {
     "heading": "Record the source behind each article",
     "paragraphs": ["An article that says what to do without saying where the rule came from is hard to defend and hard to update. Add a source line with the policy name, the system screen, or the client instruction that supports the main claim. Link to the source when possible, and record the date it was checked. If the source is a person, name the role rather than copying a private message. When the source changes, the owner can see which articles depend on it. This turns the knowledge base into a traceable record instead of a collection of confident opinions."]
    },
    {
     "heading": "Define what happens when an owner leaves",
     "paragraphs": ["Ownership rules fail most often during turnover. Before a person leaves, list every article they own, assign a temporary owner, and schedule a handover for the ones with upcoming reviews. Record the reassignment in the same place as the article. If no one can take an article, mark it unowned and set a date to resolve it rather than pretending it is covered. Review the list with the departing person while they still have access and context. A departing owner is also a chance to find articles that only that person understood."]
    },
    {
     "heading": "Measure use, not just page count",
     "paragraphs": ["A growing library is not evidence that the knowledge base works. Track searches that found an article, questions that were answered by a link, and questions that produced no useful result. Ask the team which articles they use and which ones they avoid. Review a sample of links shared in the queue and check whether the article actually answered the question. A large number of views on one article may mean it is useful or that it is confusing enough to need repeated reading. Pair usage data with a short qualitative check before drawing a conclusion."]
    },
    {
     "heading": "Retire stale articles deliberately",
     "paragraphs": ["An outdated article can be more dangerous than a missing one because it carries authority. Give the owner a retirement path: mark the article as superseded, link to the replacement, and remove it from search and navigation. Keep an archive copy for audit if the client requires it. Do not leave two articles with similar titles and different rules in the same space. When a process changes, search for every article that mentions the old step and update or retire each one. Record the retirement date so the team can see why a familiar page disappeared."]
    },
    {
     "heading": "Publish ownership where the team works",
     "paragraphs": ["Put the owner, approver, review date, and source on the article page and in the queue tool where the article is used. A separate governance spreadsheet is useful for reporting but too far from the moment of need. Add a short feedback link so a worker can flag an error without hunting for the owner. Review the ownership map in the weekly operations meeting and confirm that every high-use article has a live owner. The rule is working when a worker can find the right article, see who stands behind it, and get a correction without leaving the workflow."]
    },
   ],
   "sources": [{"name": "APQC process classification framework", "url": "https://www.apqc.org/process-frameworks", "note": "Public process framework used to structure ownership and review."}, {"name": "ISO 9001 quality management principles", "url": "https://www.iso.org/iso-9001-quality-management.html", "note": "Quality principles used to frame document control."}, {"name": "NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0", "url": "https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework", "note": "Framework used to connect access and information ownership."}, {"name": "SBA guide to hiring and managing employees", "url": "https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/manage-your-business/hire-manage-employees", "note": "Public guidance on role clarity and staff transitions."}],
   "service": "knowledge management",
   "serviceHref": "/services",
   "faqs": [{"q": "Can one person own several articles?", "a": "Yes, if the total review load is realistic. Group related articles under one owner and check the list during workload reviews so ownership does not become an invisible second job."}, {"q": "What should happen if an article has no owner?", "a": "Mark it unowned, restrict it from being cited as authority, and assign an owner by a set date. Do not leave it in the active library as if it were maintained."}]
  }
