  "title": "Design a Shadowing Week for a New Offshore Reviewer",
  "excerpt": "Structure the first week of review work with observation, graded samples, a written log, and a reverse shadow before independent decisions.",
  "minutes": 9,
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-09",
  "image": "/daily09/offshoreresourcing/design-a-shadowing-week-for-a-new-offshore-reviewer.webp",
  "details": {
   "shortAnswer": ["A shadowing week should move a new reviewer from watching decisions to making them with support, not simply sit beside someone for five days. Set a sequence of observation, paired review, graded samples, and a reverse shadow where the new reviewer explains a decision to a mentor. Require a short written log each day. Use [offshore staffing services](/services) and the [research library](/research) to connect the plan to the quality standard. End the week with a fixed evidence review and a clear decision about independent work."],
   "takeaways": ["Sequence the week from observation to supported decisions.", "Use the same graded samples for every new reviewer.", "Require a daily written log of decisions and questions.", "Finish with a reverse shadow before granting independent review."],
   "sections": [
    {
     "heading": "Start with observation before production",
     "paragraphs": ["The first day should show the new reviewer how a decision is made, not how quickly work moves. Have the mentor narrate the source record, the acceptance standard, the exception, and the reason for the final call. Ask the new reviewer to write down the questions that come up rather than interrupting every item. Review those questions at the end of the day. Observation is useful only when it is structured, so give the mentor a short list of decisions to explain. Without that structure, the week becomes passive watching and the new reviewer learns the mentor's habits rather than the standard."]
    },
    {
     "heading": "Pair the reviewer with a named mentor",
     "paragraphs": ["Name one mentor for the week and a backup for days off. The mentor should be a strong reviewer who can explain the standard, not simply the person with the most seniority. Give the mentor time in the schedule for teaching and feedback, because a full queue plus a shadow is two jobs. Introduce the pair to the queue owner and the escalation contact. The new reviewer should know who can answer a policy question and who can answer a tool question. A clear pair prevents the new person from collecting conflicting advice from everyone nearby."]
    },
    {
     "heading": "Build a graded set of samples",
     "paragraphs": ["Prepare a set of past items with known outcomes and remove sensitive details. Include normal work, a missing-field case, a borderline quality call, and an item that should have been escalated. Ask the new reviewer to make a decision and write the evidence for it before seeing the mentor's answer. Compare the two and discuss the gap. Use the same set for every new reviewer so the team can compare readiness over time. Keep the set current, and replace items when the policy or system changes enough to make the old answer wrong."]
    },
    {
     "heading": "Require a written shadow log",
     "paragraphs": ["A daily log gives the mentor evidence of what the new reviewer understood and what remained unclear. Ask for three things: one decision the reviewer would make differently, one rule that was hard to apply, and one question still open. Keep each entry short. The mentor reviews the log before the next session and corrects misunderstandings while they are fresh. At the end of the week, the log becomes part of the readiness review. It also protects the new reviewer, because it shows that questions were raised and answered rather than hidden."]
    },
    {
     "heading": "Add a reverse shadow step",
     "paragraphs": ["In a reverse shadow, the new reviewer makes the decision while the mentor watches and asks questions. This is the first real test of independent judgment because the new person must explain the evidence and the rule without prompting. Use low-risk live items or realistic samples. Ask the reviewer to state the acceptance condition, the evidence inspected, and the reason for the call. The mentor should intervene only when the decision could cause harm or when a rule is clearly misunderstood. Record the level of support required for each item."]
    },
    {
     "heading": "Review the week against fixed criteria",
     "paragraphs": ["Before the week starts, write what a successful shadow looks like. Use criteria such as correct application of the acceptance standard, accurate evidence citation, appropriate escalation, and a complete review note. Do not use vague qualities such as attitude or potential. At the end of the week, the mentor and the queue owner compare the log, the graded samples, and the reverse-shadow notes against the criteria. Record the decision: independent review, another week of support, or a narrower scope. Give the new reviewer the same evidence and a chance to respond."]
    },
    {
     "heading": "Avoid shadowing as unpaid extra work",
     "paragraphs": ["Shadowing is training and should be recognized as work time, including the reading, log writing, and feedback sessions. Check the applicable labor rules and the contract before scheduling extra hours. Do not ask a new reviewer to practice off the clock or to cover a full queue while learning. Build the mentor's reduced review load into the plan so the teaching is not squeezed into gaps. If the schedule cannot support the time, shorten the live scope rather than skipping the training. A rushed shadow creates a false sense of readiness."]
    },
    {
     "heading": "Plan the first independent week",
     "paragraphs": ["The week after the shadow should still be protected. Give the new reviewer a limited queue, a named backup, and a short daily check with the mentor. Review a sample of their decisions before the items leave the team. Increase volume only after the sample shows consistent evidence and escalation. Keep the shadow log open so the reviewer can record new questions. At the end of the first independent week, compare the sample with the shadow criteria and decide whether the support can be reduced. Independence should be earned with evidence, not granted by the calendar."]
    },
   ],
   "sources": [{"name": "SBA guide to hiring and managing employees", "url": "https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/manage-your-business/hire-manage-employees", "note": "Public guidance on onboarding and performance management."}, {"name": "DOL Fact Sheet 22: Hours Worked", "url": "https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/22-flsa-hours-worked", "note": "Primary guidance on compensable training time."}, {"name": "ISO 9001 quality management principles", "url": "https://www.iso.org/iso-9001-quality-management.html", "note": "Quality principles used to frame reviewer competence."}, {"name": "APQC process classification framework", "url": "https://www.apqc.org/process-frameworks", "note": "Public process framework used to structure the shadowing sequence."}],
   "service": "training and onboarding",
   "serviceHref": "/services",
   "faqs": [{"q": "How long should a shadow last?", "a": "One week is a useful default for a defined review role, but the decision should depend on the evidence. Complex or high-consequence work may need a second supported week before independent review."}, {"q": "Should the mentor be the new reviewer's manager?", "a": "Usually not. The mentor should be close enough to the work to explain decisions, while the manager owns the readiness decision and the personnel record."}]
  }
