  "title": "Plan Knowledge Transfer from a Departing Onshore Manager",
  "excerpt": "Capture the decisions, exceptions, and informal calendar that a departing manager carries before the last week arrives.",
  "minutes": 9,
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-09",
  "image": "/daily09/offshoreresourcing/plan-knowledge-transfer-from-a-departing-onshore-manager.webp",
  "details": {
   "shortAnswer": ["Knowledge transfer from a departing onshore manager should start with the decisions only that person makes, not with a generic list of tasks. Capture recurring exceptions, the informal calendar, the client relationships, and the evidence behind current rules. Assign a receiving owner early and test the handover on live items before the last day. Use [offshore staffing services](/services) and the [research library](/research) to connect the plan to role coverage. Keep a short question window open after the departure so the team can resolve gaps."],
   "takeaways": ["Inventory decisions and exceptions, not just recurring tasks.", "Assign the receiving owner before the final two weeks.", "Test the handover with live items and no rescue from the departing manager.", "Record where the current rules came from, including informal agreements."],
   "sections": [
    {
     "heading": "Inventory the decisions only that manager makes",
     "paragraphs": ["Start with a list of decisions that currently stop at the departing manager. Include approvals, client commitments, scope changes, escalations, and exceptions to the normal process. Ask the manager to name the decision, the trigger, the evidence used, the people consulted, and the usual turnaround. Then ask the team which decisions they actually route to that person, because the formal list often misses the informal ones. A transfer that covers only the org chart will leave the real bottlenecks in place. Rank the decisions by frequency and consequence so the limited handover time goes to the highest-risk items."]
    },
    {
     "heading": "Capture recurring exceptions, not just tasks",
     "paragraphs": ["The most valuable knowledge often sits in the exceptions: the client who always needs a different format, the invoice that needs a second signature, the report that is late because a source system refreshes overnight. Ask the manager to walk through the last twenty exceptions and explain the pattern behind each one. Record the condition, the approved response, and the person who can confirm it. Turn repeated exceptions into written rules where possible, and flag the rest as judgment calls with a named backup. This reduces the chance that the receiver inherits a pile of undocumented habits."]
    },
    {
     "heading": "Record the informal calendar",
     "paragraphs": ["A departing manager carries a calendar that may not appear in any shared system. It includes the client review that always happens on the first Tuesday, the report that must be filed before a regulatory date, and the quiet week when the client is closed. Put those events into a shared calendar with an owner and a preparation note. Mark which ones are fixed and which are flexible. Ask the manager to identify the events that would cause real damage if missed. The receiving owner should shadow at least one full cycle of the most important recurring event before the departure."]
    },
    {
     "heading": "Assign a receiving owner before the last week",
     "paragraphs": ["Name the person who will take over each decision and tell the client and the team. A transfer that starts in the final week becomes a series of rushed conversations with no time to test understanding. Give the receiver access early enough to practice while the manager can still correct them. Record the transfer in a simple table with the decision, the receiver, the backup, the evidence, and the date confirmed. If no receiver exists for a decision, escalate that gap instead of assuming the team will absorb it. An unassigned decision is the clearest sign that the handover plan is incomplete."]
    },
    {
     "heading": "Run a live handover on real items",
     "paragraphs": ["Use live or recent items rather than a slide deck. Ask the receiver to make the decision while the manager observes and then compare the reasoning. Do this for a normal case, an exception, and a case where the rule is unclear. Record where the receiver needed help and what was missing from the written instructions. The manager should correct the instruction, not just the individual answer. Repeat the exercise until the receiver can handle the common cases without prompting. Live practice exposes gaps that a conversation about the process will never reveal."]
    },
    {
     "heading": "Test the receiver without the manager",
     "paragraphs": ["Before the last day, run a short period where the manager is available only for emergencies. The receiver handles the decisions, the client communication, and the exceptions. The manager reviews the outcome afterward rather than intervening in real time. This test shows whether the receiver can find the evidence, reach the right people, and make a timely call. Record the issues and fix the access, the contact list, or the instruction. If the test fails, reduce the receiver's scope or extend the support rather than declaring the handover complete on schedule."]
    },
    {
     "heading": "Preserve access and document ownership",
     "paragraphs": ["List the systems, folders, distribution lists, and client portals the manager uses, and transfer ownership before the account is disabled. Check that the receiver can open the files, approve the requests, and receive the alerts. Remove the departing manager only after the transfer is confirmed. Record the new owner in the document and the system, not only in the handover notes. If a client requires a named contact, update that record with the client. Access transfer is part of knowledge transfer because knowledge trapped behind a disabled account is effectively lost."]
    },
    {
     "heading": "Set a 30-day question window",
     "paragraphs": ["Offer the team a defined window after the departure for questions, with a named person who can answer them. Keep the window short enough to be realistic and long enough to cover the first monthly cycle. Log each question and the answer, then add the useful ones to the knowledge base or the process notes. Review the log at the end of the window and identify the topics that still lack a written answer. Close the window with a short summary of what was resolved and what remains a risk. This turns a temporary courtesy into a durable improvement."]
    },
   ],
   "sources": [{"name": "SBA guide to hiring and managing employees", "url": "https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/manage-your-business/hire-manage-employees", "note": "Public guidance on staffing transitions and management continuity."}, {"name": "APQC process classification framework", "url": "https://www.apqc.org/process-frameworks", "note": "Process framework used to structure decision transfer."}, {"name": "NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0", "url": "https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework", "note": "Framework used to frame access transfer and ownership."}, {"name": "ISO 9001 quality management principles", "url": "https://www.iso.org/iso-9001-quality-management.html", "note": "Quality principles used to frame documented knowledge."}],
   "service": "knowledge management",
   "serviceHref": "/services",
   "faqs": [{"q": "How early should the handover start?", "a": "At least four to six weeks before the last day for a manager with client and approval duties. High-risk or highly informal roles may need longer."}, {"q": "What if the departing manager is not cooperative?", "a": "Escalate to the owner of the role and document the gaps. Use the team, the client record, and the system history to reconstruct as much as possible, and assign a named owner to each remaining risk."}]
  }
